19.2.content:
Either party may terminate this SOW for cause in the event of the other party’s material breach of this SOW or the governing agreement applicable to this SOW, subject to `<LEGAL: confirm breach-notice and cure-period requirements under the governing agreement>`.

Without limiting any rights available under the governing agreement, termination for cause may include circumstances involving:

- material breach of this SOW or the governing agreement that is not cured within the applicable cure period;
- insolvency, bankruptcy, liquidation, assignment for the benefit of creditors, or cessation of business by a party, to the extent such grounds are permitted under applicable law and the governing agreement;
- fraud, willful misconduct, or unlawful conduct by a party in connection with performance of this SOW, to the extent termination on such basis is permitted under the governing agreement;
- repeated failure by a party to perform a material obligation under this SOW, where such failure has a material adverse effect on the approved `COT Supply` scope and is not cured in accordance with the governing agreement; and
- `<LEGAL: confirm any additional cause-based termination grounds expressly provided in the governing agreement that should be referenced here>`.

A party seeking to terminate this SOW for cause shall provide written notice to the other party describing the basis for termination in reasonable detail, subject to the notice requirements of the governing agreement. If the applicable breach or event is curable, the non-breaching party shall allow the contractually required cure period before termination becomes effective.

Where the basis for termination relates to a delivery issue, dependency failure, milestone delay, acceptance dispute, access delay, or other execution matter that may be capable of operational resolution within the approved governance structure for the `COT Supply` engagement, the parties shall first use the agreed escalation and governance process to the extent consistent with the governing agreement and the severity of the issue. However, no project-governance discussion, escalation activity, or change-control discussion shall limit either party’s right to exercise a valid termination-for-cause remedy where the contractual conditions for such remedy have been satisfied.

For clarity:

- no party may rely on informal discussions, draft comments, workshop discussions, or unresolved working-level disagreements alone as a basis for termination for cause unless such matters rise to the level of a contractual cause event under this SOW or the governing agreement;
- no delay, dependency issue, missed review cycle, or disputed delivery matter shall by itself constitute cause for termination unless it constitutes a material breach or other termination ground under the governing agreement and any applicable cure requirements have been satisfied;
- where a claimed cause event arises from disputed scope, deferred decisions, unapproved change requests, client-side dependency failure, or matters outside the affected party’s contractual responsibility, the parties shall assess such matter in light of the approved scope, obligations, assumptions, exclusions, and change-control provisions of this SOW and the governing agreement; and
- termination for cause under this SOW shall be exercised only by an authorized representative of the terminating party and in accordance with the formal notice and approval requirements of the governing agreement.

If termination for cause becomes effective, the consequences of such termination, including payment treatment, treatment of work in progress, return or destruction of confidential information, access revocation, ownership and handoff of deliverables, survival of obligations, and any further remedies, shall be governed by Section 19.3 of this SOW and the governing agreement.

This subsection is intended to align with, and not replace, narrow, or expand beyond, the termination-for-cause rights and remedies set out in the governing agreement. Any cause-based termination right, limitation, procedural requirement, insolvency-related restriction, fraud-related remedy, or post-termination consequence not expressly stated here shall be governed by the governing agreement.
